REL_EN = ['<li><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.19606">Hyper-Connections, Zhu et al. 2024</a>, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.24880">mHC, Xie et al. 2025</a>, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.15031">Attention Residuals, Kimi Team 2026</a> and <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.12170">MUDDFormer, Xiao et al. 2025</a> each report that removing the learned routing raises the loss: by 0.021 at 16 layers, 0.022 at a size not stated, 0.012 at 16 blocks and 0.35 perplexity at 24 layers. Our 12-layer pair agrees with them in size, and the sign flips by 48 layers (Figure 51).</li>',
          '<li><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.02622">DenseFormer, Pagliardini et al. 2024</a> finds that fixed weights over all earlier layers lower perplexity by 0.77 at 48 blocks. <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.05732">mHC-lite, Yang and Gao 2026</a> finds that unconstrained Hyper-Connections loses its gain by 24 layers. <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.32534">DepthBench, Wang et al. 2026</a> finds that Hyper-Connections keeps gaining from depth up to 32 layers with AdamW. None of them trains the same design with its routing removed at 48 layers.</li>']
